WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.616
A linear system can contain quantities changing at different speeds

00:00:04.616 --> 00:00:09.254
Here the y component decays four times as quickly as x

00:00:09.254 --> 00:00:16.000
A time-stepping method must respect that faster scale even when the exact solutions themselves behave smoothly.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:21.916
Forward Euler uses the current derivative to predict the next value

00:00:21.916 --> 00:00:26.952
With step size one quarter, the fast component is multiplied by zero

00:00:26.952 --> 00:00:33.667
This is an approximation, not the exact decay; the slow component is multiplied by three quarters.

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:38.262
Choose a step size of three quarters instead

00:00:38.262 --> 00:00:43.617
The fast component now changes sign and doubles in magnitude at each update

00:00:43.617 --> 00:00:47.324
The true solution decays, but the numerical solution grows

00:00:47.324 --> 00:00:52.267
Stability requires the magnitude of its update factor to stay below one.

00:00:53.567 --> 00:01:00.426
Backward Euler evaluates the derivative at the unknown next value, producing a linear equation to solve

00:01:00.426 --> 00:01:08.571
The fast component is multiplied by one divided by one plus four h, which contracts for every positive h

00:01:08.571 --> 00:01:12.000
Stability still does not guarantee a desired accuracy.
